From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:14:24 +0000
Subject: Drop the SandboxStatusExtension binder when its frame goes away

On Android every frame gets a SandboxStatusExtension, a ref-counted
RenderFrameObserver. For main frames its constructor registers an
associated interface binder that holds a reference to the object, so the
Release() in OnDestruct() does not free it. RenderFrameImpl's destructor
then calls RenderFrameGone() on every observer that is still alive, which
clears the observer's frame pointer, and only later destroys its members.
Destroying the associated interface registry drops the binder's reference
and frees the extension. Its RenderFrameObserver destructor no longer
knows the frame, so it does not remove itself from the frame's observer
list. The list then holds a pointer to freed memory when it is destroyed,
and with dangling pointer detection in crash mode the renderer stops
every time a main frame is destroyed: on network error pages, on some
cross-site navigations and when a tab is closed.

Remove the binder from the registry in OnDestruct(), before the final
Release(). The object is then freed while the frame still tracks it and
removes itself from the observer list as usual. A reply still in flight
keeps its own reference, as before, and finds no frame when it runs.

